      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine after how many bounces the ball first rises to a height less than 30 cm, we need to understand the sequence of heights the ball reaches after each bounce. The ball initially falls from a height of 243 cm and bounces back to \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) of that height with each subsequent bounce.\n\nLet's calculate the height of the ball after each bounce:\n\n1. After the first bounce:\n   \\[\n   h_1 = 243 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 162 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n\n2. After the second bounce:\n   \\[\n   h_2 = 162 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 108 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n\n3. After the third bounce:\n   \\[\n   h_3 = 108 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 72 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n\n4. After the fourth bounce:\n   \\[\n   h_4 = 72 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 48 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n\n5. After the fifth bounce:\n   \\[\n   h_5 = 48 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 32 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n\n6. After the sixth bounce:\n   \\[\n   h_6 = 32 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{64}{3} \\approx 21.33 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n\nWe see that after the fifth bounce, the height of the ball is 32 cm, which is less than 30 cm. Therefore, the ball first rises to a height less than 30 cm after 5 bounces.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]",
