      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine after how many bounces the ball first rises to a height less than 30 cm, we start by noting that the ball bounces back to $\\frac{2}{3}$ of its previous height with each fall. The initial height from which the ball is dropped is 243 cm. We can express the height after each bounce using the formula:\n\n\\[\nh_n = 243 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n\n\\]\n\nwhere $h_n$ is the height after the $n$-th bounce. We need to find the smallest integer $n$ such that $h_n < 30$ cm. Therefore, we set up the inequality:\n\n\\[\n243 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < 30\n\\]\n\nFirst, we isolate the exponential term by dividing both sides of the inequality by 243:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < \\frac{30}{243}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right side of the inequality:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < \\frac{10}{81}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for $n$, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n\\[\n\\ln \\left(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\right) < \\ln \\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right)\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms that $\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)$, we get:\n\n\\[\nn \\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) < \\ln \\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSince $\\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$ is a negative number, we divide both sides by $\\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$ and reverse the inequality:\n\n\\[\nn > \\frac{\\ln \\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right)}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to calculate the values of the logarithms. Using a calculator, we find:\n\n\\[\n\\ln \\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right) \\approx -1.8335\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\approx -0.4055\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\nn > \\frac{-1.8335}{-0.4055} \\approx 4.53\n\\]\n\nSince $n$ must be an integer, we round up to the next whole number. Therefore, $n = 5$. To confirm, we can check the height after the 4th and 5th bounces:\n\nFor $n = 4$:\n\n\\[\nh_4 = 243 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^4 = 243 \\left(\\frac{16}{81}\\right) = 48 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nFor $n = 5$:\n\n\\[\nh_5 = 243 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^5 = 243 \\left(\\frac{32}{243}\\right) = 32 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nSince $h_5 = 32$ cm is less than 30 cm, the ball first rises to a height less than 30 cm after 5 bounces. Therefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]",
